      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.1 A research using behavioural insight approach conducted to identify means for preventing and responding to GBV in line with the human rights principles

1.	The final version of the research proposal has been developed and approved by the Behavioral Insights team.  The Behavioural Insights research proposal paves the foundation in research implementation that focuses on exploring the barriers to help-seeking faced by survivors of gender-based violence (GBV) in DKI Jakarta Province, Indonesia. The study seeks to: 1) understand help seeking behaviour of GBV survivors in areas with high rates of GBV cases; 2) describe the patterns of help-seeking; and establish the challenges and barriers faced by survivors of GBV in seeking help.

Output: 2.1. Data collection and analysis of BI approach and findings are ongoing and expected to be completed in January 2023

The recruitment of a consultant to provide advisory and the recruitment of 3 senior field researchers have been completed in October 2022. UNDP conducted a two-day briefing programme in November 2022 and invited the Research Team, together with the BIT. The 2 day briefing consists of Technical Briefing and Psychological Approach Briefing. Data collection is confirmed to start during late November to early December 2023. In addition, contract amendment with BIT was initiated in November 2022 with BRH to include analytical service to the findings from the research.
Awareness raising on BI approach and GBV service provision at Urban 20 side event

UNDP recognizes the Urban 20 (U20) as a powerful knowledge sharing platform for city leaders to collaborate in addressing socio-economic and environmental barriers to foster sustainable and inclu- sive economic and social recoveries post COVID 19. Against this background, the U20 played a pivotal and triggering role for gearing up further political attention and commitment to increase protection for women, children and other vulnerable groups from the threats of Gender Based Violence by the international community at large. U20 and its members could promote more policy coherence at international level, more effective cooperation and stronger technical capability, inter alia by advo- cating evidence-based recommendations, information and valuable elements on GBV prevention and eradication towards its own constituency, as well as to larger audiences. Recognizing the critical needs to address GBV in promoting inclusive social recovery for all, UNDP and the Government of DKI Jakarta jointly facilitated knowledge sharing and outreach at U20 2022 side event on 30 August 2022 at Fairmont Hotel. The side event also served as information sharing on the the use of BI approach as innovative initiave to promote behavior change, while also maximising outreach to strategic stakeholders on integrated service center as part of UNDP GBV prevention and eradication in the country.</narrative>
